From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 15:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] kk: support 64-bit image atomics

Metal's texture templates declare a void atomic_max/atomic_min taking a ulong4
on texture2d<ulong> (and the other dims) backed by R32G32_UINT, and no other
64-bit texture atomic: no fetch form, add/and/or/xor, exchange or
compare-exchange, and no signed variant. That is the same shape as the buffer
case in "kk: support 64-bit buffer atomics".

Map VK_FORMAT_R64_UINT/R64_SINT to R32G32_UINT as storage-image (atomic) and
transfer formats, advertise VK_EXT_shader_image_atomic_int64
(shaderImageInt64Atomics; sparseImageInt64Atomics stays false) and lower
OpAtomicUMax/OpAtomicUMin on a 64-bit image to the void texture form, preceded
by a level-0 texel read when the result is used. The effect on memory is exactly
Vulkan's; the returned value is the texel read just before the operation, with
the same caveat as the buffer path. Every other 64-bit image atomic is rejected
before MSL is built (nir_uses_unsupported_64bit_atomics).

Unreal Engine 5's D3D12 SM6 path (Nanite's visibility buffer) requires 64-bit
typed-resource atomics, which vkd3d-proton derives from shaderImageInt64Atomics;
without them UE5 titles report "DirectX 12 is not supported on your system".

MESA_KK_DEBUG=noimageint64atomics stops advertising it.

Tested with host/moltenvk/repro/image_atomic64.c: 32768 invocations racing on
64-bit image min/max with the CPU recomputing every texel, and the uncontended
fetch result checked exactly. The Metal operation itself was verified on an
M5 Max with steamac-dev/scripts/mtl_tex_atomic64.swift.

